{"name":"Fresh Water","hex":"#c6e3f7","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fresh water","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water with a very low content of dissolved salt, as opposed to brackish water or salt water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fresh_water"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fresh water","blocks":[{"text":"Fresh water or freshwater is any naturally occurring liquid or frozen water containing low concentrations of dissolved salts and other total dissolved solids. The term excludes seawater and brackish water, but it does include non-salty mineral-rich waters, such as chalybeate springs. Fresh water may encompass frozen and meltwater in ice sheets, ice caps, glaciers, snowfields and icebergs, natural precipitations such as rainfall, snowfall, hail/sleet and graupel, and surface runoffs that form inland bodies of water such as wetlands, ponds, lakes, rivers, streams, as well as groundwater contained in aquifers, subterranean rivers and lakes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Amazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_%2811472506936%29.jpg/330px-Amazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_%2811472506936%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fresh_water","wikidata":"Q102192"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Amazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_%2811472506936%29.jpg/500px-Amazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_%2811472506936%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Amazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_%2811472506936%29.jpg/1280px-Amazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_%2811472506936%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Amazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_%2811472506936%29.jpg/500px-Amazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_%2811472506936%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmazonas%2C_Iquitos_-_Leticia%2C_Kolumbien_(11472506936).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Cove_with_Shamans_Rock_Olkhon_Island_Lake_Baikal_Russia_%2814594856552%29.jpg/500px-Cove_with_Shamans_Rock_Olkhon_Island_Lake_Baikal_Russia_%2814594856552%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Cove_with_Shamans_Rock_Olkhon_Island_Lake_Baikal_Russia_%2814594856552%29.jpg/1280px-Cove_with_Shamans_Rock_Olkhon_Island_Lake_Baikal_Russia_%2814594856552%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo